From: Andrea Mattera <26578395+m0s4ik@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: q6asm: reset the buffer index and hw pointer when mapping

Not upstream: carried by thinksmart-view-debian (driver/q6asm). Found by
reading the code, not yet confirmed on hardware.

Each port of an audio client keeps two pieces of state about the ring
it shares with the DSP: dsp_buf, the index of the next buffer handed to
the DSP by q6asm_write_async() or q6asm_read(), and hw_ptr, the number
of the last buffer the DSP returned, which q6asm-dai reports as the
position. Neither is reset when the ring is mapped again.

q6asm-dai unmaps and maps the ring on every prepare of a stream that
already ran. dsp_buf is reset only by CMD_FLUSH, which the PCM path
never sends, and hw_ptr only changes when a buffer comes back. So after
a re-prepare:

 - ALSA writes at ring offset 0 while the driver queues buf[k], where k
   is the buffer the previous run stopped at: the DSP plays (or, on
   capture, fills) the ring from the wrong place. If the new
   hw_params has fewer periods than k, port->buf[port->dsp_buf] reads
   past the end of the new buffer array.
 - q6asm_dai_pointer() reports the previous run's position until the
   first buffer returns, and then k+1 instead of 1.

The expected effect is a burst of stale audio and/or an immediate
spurious xrun after every restart that did not stop on a lap boundary;
on capture, up to k stale periods are delivered after a restart.

Reset both when the ring is mapped, under the client lock. q6apm got
the same fixes in cab45ab95ce7 (dsp_buf in q6apm_map_memory_regions)
and 0337b8129cf6 (hw_ptr).

Fixes: 44432315444c ("ASoC: qcom: q6asm-dai: use q6asm_get_hw_pointer")
---
 sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6asm.c
@@ -500,6 +500,8 @@
 	int rc;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&ac->lock, flags);
+	ac->port[dir].dsp_buf = 0;
+	atomic_set(&ac->port[dir].hw_ptr, 0);
 	if (ac->port[dir].buf) {
 		dev_err(ac->dev, "Buffer already allocated\n");
 		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ac->lock, flags);
